WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.735 --> 00:00:01.985
FELIPE HOFFA: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.985 --> 00:00:04.860
I am Felipe Hoffa, Google
Cloud Developer Advocate,

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.700
and today, I'm in
Johannesburg, South Africa,

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.890
to meet the CTO of
Nomanini, Dale Humby.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.760
This company enables
micro-transactions

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.040
in various countries
around Africa.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.460
With these devices, they create.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.040
Dale is going to tell us
more about their story

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.560
and why they chose to build
their backbone with Google

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:23.490
Cloud.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.970
Also, we'll show you how you
can use open data and BigQuery

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.490
to visualize the state of
the mobile networks that

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.650
connect the world.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.720
So join me.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.560
It's my first time
in South Africa.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.670
And I think there's so much
we can learn about Africa just

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.290
by looking at what you're doing.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.850
Not only South
Africa, but Africa.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:42.975
DALE HUMBY: That's correct.

00:00:42.975 --> 00:00:46.950
So what Nomanini does is we
build point-of-sale systems

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.470
to help remote
villagers get access

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:53.430
to prepaid airtime, prepaid
electricity, and basic banking

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.399
services like being able to
withdraw or deposit cash.

00:00:56.399 --> 00:00:57.190
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:01.300
And Nomanini got into this
business just to help people?

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.100
DALE HUMBY: Yes.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.810
Back in 2011 when we
started the company,

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.350
we focused a lot
more on air time.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.280
We wanted to get scratchcards
out into rural villages,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.350
but it's very
difficult and expensive

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.260
to send boxes of
scratchcards, which are worth

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.550
money, into far-flung villages
and then collect cash and bring

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.560
it back to the cities to
pay for that air time.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.130
If you wirelessly deliver
air time, these vouchers,

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.580
to machines that
are in the village,

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.979
it's a lot cheaper and
more cost effective.

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:36.270
So people in the villages
who don't have a lot of money

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.965
to start with don't have
to travel far to go and get

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:42.370
the essential services like
air time or electricity,

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.834
and now even banking services.

00:01:44.834 --> 00:01:46.500
FELIPE HOFFA: So as
the CTO of Nomanini,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.094
you created all of these
devices back in 2011?

00:01:50.094 --> 00:01:50.760
DALE HUMBY: Yes.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.390
We started off with a terminal.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.370
It was very simple to use--
no screen, just some buttons

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.580
where you press the mobile
network like Vodafone,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:04.380
and you press the 10 rand
denomination or [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.140
Meti Cash, wherever you
are on the continent.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.449
And it prints out a slip,
and you got a PIN number

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:11.125
that you type into your phone.

00:02:11.125 --> 00:02:13.280
FELIPE HOFFA: And then
you started with the cloud

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:14.420
back in 2011.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.110
DALE HUMBY: Yeah.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.110
Before I joined Nomanini,
I had my own company

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.810
and we were doing the early
days of internet of things.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:24.120
And I absolutely fell
in love with App Engine

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.370
because I'm more of an
embedded system guy,

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.820
so what I knew back then about
setting up high availability

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.920
servers was a bit scary.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.720
So App Engine solved my problem.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:38.280
And then when I joined Nomanini,
I took that knowledge with me

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.890
and we built up our
systems backend,

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.190
that does all the
transaction processing

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.960
and the maintenance
and monitoring of all

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.030
these devices, on App Engine.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.070
FELIPE HOFFA: Now, to be
able to pull this off,

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.230
you need to have
network connectivity.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.190
DALE HUMBY: Yes, that's correct.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:56.004
FELIPE HOFFA: How's
that working in Africa?

00:02:56.004 --> 00:02:57.420
DALE HUMBY: So
each of our devices

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.840
has a SIM card in them that
links up to the network

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:04.410
and of course a REST
API on our system.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.280
But the networks,
while they're there,

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.650
they can be a little bit flaky.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.480
So knowing what the
signal strength thing is

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.010
is very important for us.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.350
FELIPE HOFFA: How can
I learn more about it?

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:17.040
DALE HUMBY: Well, there's a
project called the OpenCellID

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:17.895
Project.

00:03:17.895 --> 00:03:22.320
And people with GPSes
and data monitors--

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.570
typically tracking companies--

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.240
take their data and
anonymize it and upload it

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.690
to this open-source
project called OpenCellID.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.960
And this project then takes
that data and figures out

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.472
where all the base stations
are around the world.

00:03:36.472 --> 00:03:37.930
FELIPE HOFFA: Can
you show me that?

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:38.090
DALE HUMBY: Absolutely.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:38.910
FELIPE HOFFA: This
sounds fascinating.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:39.960
DALE HUMBY: Yeah.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.810
So I've got this page open here.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.100
This is the OpenCellID
page, and you

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.550
can see all those base
stations around the world

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:49.830
that they've collected data for.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:56.340
I've registered on the
API, so if I log in then

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.900
I can go to Download and can
download the latest cell tower

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.450
data from this link.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.250
And what I did--

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.560
I didn't want to download
it onto my laptop

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.600
and then re-upload
it into Google Cloud

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.460
Storage or BigQuery.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.400
So I spun up an f1-micro,
which is a free instance,

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.600
and I used CURL to
download the file which

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.940
is about 800 megs or so.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.780
And then from there, I
used gsutil to upload it

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.240
from that f1-micro instance
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.670
So here you can see
the cell phone towers--

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.612
886 megs of gzip data.

00:04:32.612 --> 00:04:34.570
FELIPE HOFFA: And if you
want to analyze this--

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:35.278
DALE HUMBY: Yeah.

00:04:35.278 --> 00:04:37.650
Well, it's no use just like
sitting in Google Cloud

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.310
Storage, so I used BigQuery.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:41.520
So one of my favorite
tools with BigQuery

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.130
for importing or
creating a table

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.170
is you can point it straight at
a Google Cloud Storage bucket,

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:50.340
and you can point
it to a gzip file.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.380
You don't even need to worry
about unzipping it first.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.980
I told it it's a CSV format
and I know all the columns.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.490
FELIPE HOFFA: Radio, MCC, net--

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:03.920
DALE HUMBY: Radio, so
it's like GSM or LTE.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:05.919
MCC-- Mobile Country
Code, so that's

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:06.960
the country that it's in.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.680
The network is like
Vodacom, MTN, whatever.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.100
Area is like the suburb, and
cell is the unique cell ID.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.099
And from there, I imported it,
and so I've got those data here

00:05:18.099 --> 00:05:19.890
so you can see all the
columns it imported.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.170
And if I look at
the details, it's

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:27.680
4 gigs, so it went from 880 megs
to 4 gigabytes decompressed.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:32.130
And it's got just shy of 40
million unique base stations

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.400
from around the world.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.320
I can then have a look at,
for instance, this one--

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.850
is finding out the latitude
and the longitude and the radio

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:42.510
type.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:45.690
So having a look at,
basically, where's GSM?

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.880
Where is the LTE networks?

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.100
How is the world moving
from first gen, second gen,

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.230
third gen, fourth gen
cell phone towers?

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:55.460
So running this query,
if I run it again--

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.210
FELIPE HOFFA: So you're
grouping here by--

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:58.620
DALE HUMBY: Grouping it by the--

00:05:58.620 --> 00:05:59.680
FELIPE HOFFA: --the
latitude and longitude?

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.554
DALE HUMBY: --the latitude
and the longitude.

00:06:01.554 --> 00:06:04.380
So that's just doing some
rounding with that division

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.910
there just so we don't
get too many data points,

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:10.170
and then looking at the
case when it's GSM or LTE.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.170
And then importing
that, or at least

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:16.664
visualizing that,
inside of Data Studio.

00:06:16.664 --> 00:06:19.080
So I took those four network
types that they've recorded--

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:22.830
CDMA, which is
primarily, you can see,

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.540
used in the US and
some in the east.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:28.500
The world has moved on to GSM.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:32.190
So throughout the world,
we've got GSM everywhere.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.370
And you can see,
particularly in Africa,

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:38.010
is huge amount of coverage
with GSM networks there.

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:42.330
Then we move over to third gen,
so this is like your 3G data.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:46.370
Obviously also widely rolled
out, but less so in Africa,

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:49.500
but there's still quite
a lot of 3G available.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.040
And then we move over to the
fourth-gen networks, so LTE.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.650
Also some in Africa,
mostly in the big cities.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:00.930
And huge in the east,
so LTE is big there.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:02.940
Africa's got some
catching up to do.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:04.854
And that took me about
five minutes to do.

00:07:04.854 --> 00:07:05.770
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.430
You downloaded all of this
data, you load it into BigQuery,

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.695
and suddenly we're looking
at the whole world--

00:07:10.695 --> 00:07:12.210
how antennas are looking.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:13.579
DALE HUMBY: That's right.

00:07:13.579 --> 00:07:15.120
FELIPE HOFFA: Can
we focus on Africa?

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:17.040
What else can you
see about Africa?

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:17.748
DALE HUMBY: Sure.

00:07:17.748 --> 00:07:22.050
So what I had a look at was,
given the mobile country

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:22.672
codes--

00:07:22.672 --> 00:07:23.880
FELIPE HOFFA: That's the MCC?

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:24.930
DALE HUMBY: That's the MCC--

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:25.930
the mobile country code.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:27.750
So each country
has a unique code,

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:30.210
like South Africa's is 655.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:34.877
And we did a join with the
mobile country codes with

00:07:34.877 --> 00:07:36.960
country names-- so there's
a database that has all

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:38.790
the country names and
the country codes--

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.790
and then doing a count to have
a look at how many antennas,

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:44.790
unique base stations, for
every country in the world.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:48.990
And taking that, and I wanted to
explore that a little bit more,

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:51.420
so putting in the
ISO code, which

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:56.470
is like ZA for South Africa, or
GB for Great Britain, or Puerto

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:56.970
Rico--

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:58.020
PR.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.520
And then going even
one step further,

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:04.000
joining that with one
of Google's country info

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.610
databases that's
available on the internet.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:10.800
So having a look at population
data versus how many antennas,

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.350
and seeing, is there a
correlation between population

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:16.421
size and how much a mobile
network has rolled out.

00:08:16.421 --> 00:08:18.670
FELIPE HOFFA: How many
antennas do we have per people?

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:19.830
DALE HUMBY: Exactly, exactly.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.538
And that's this division
here, the number

00:08:21.538 --> 00:08:23.700
of antennas per capita.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.370
And this is actually
per 1,000 people,

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:30.820
so like Australia has got 17
antennas for 1,000 people,

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:31.320
right?

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:34.500
So if we once again
go into Data Studio,

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:37.659
we can have a look at what
that looks like across Africa.

00:08:37.659 --> 00:08:40.440
And if we plot that
on a log-log scale

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.210
of the total antennas
versus the total population,

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:46.210
here you can a look.

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:50.070
See with the big
countries, it correlates

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:52.950
with having lots of
mobile base stations,

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:55.620
and the smaller countries
with less population

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:57.670
have fewer bases, which
sort of makes sense.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:08:59.272
So there's this linear trend.

00:08:59.272 --> 00:09:01.230
FELIPE HOFFA: But you
still see a big disparity

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:03.139
where some countries
have a lot of antennas.

00:09:03.139 --> 00:09:04.180
DALE HUMBY: That's right.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:05.630
If you look, this
is a log scale.

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:07.546
So have a look at country
like the Seychelles.

00:09:07.546 --> 00:09:10.230
It's got very few
people, at 88,000,

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:14.310
but they've got just under
1,000 antennas there.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.976
And other countries
like Chad, which

00:09:16.976 --> 00:09:18.600
is right in the middle
of Africa here--

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:19.200
FELIPE HOFFA: That's
the light spot.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.440
DALE HUMBY: The light spot--

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:24.360
they've got quite a high
population but not widely

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.112
rolled-out mobile network.

00:09:26.112 --> 00:09:27.320
And the countries that have--

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.895
South Africa, Nigeria, Egypt--

00:09:29.895 --> 00:09:31.600
and you look up at the top here.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.550
There's Nigeria,
Egypt, South Africa.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:37.680
So for South Africa's
population of just under 50

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:40.680
million people, we've got
the most widely rolled-out

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.420
mobile network in Africa.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.990
FELIPE HOFFA: And in which
countries is Nomanini?

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:47.580
DALE HUMBY: Nomanini is
operating a little bit

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.420
in South Africa, but mostly
in Mozambique, and Zambia,

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:53.820
and Kenya, and a little
bit in Ghana, as well.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:55.236
FELIPE HOFFA: And
here you can get

00:09:55.236 --> 00:09:58.020
a look of where you are
getting real coverage reported

00:09:58.020 --> 00:09:58.815
by people.

00:09:58.815 --> 00:09:59.856
DALE HUMBY: That's right.

00:09:59.856 --> 00:10:00.370
Yeah.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:01.161
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:10:01.161 --> 00:10:02.930
And then you can take
all of this data--

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:04.210
this is public data.

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:04.575
DALE HUMBY: That's right.

00:10:04.575 --> 00:10:05.940
FELIPE HOFFA: And you can
combine it with your own data.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:06.648
DALE HUMBY: Yeah.

00:10:06.648 --> 00:10:09.890
So we have a bunch
of diagnostic data

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:12.020
that's uploaded to our systems.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:13.520
So if I have a look
at one of ours--

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:17.330
this is a particular
user in Mozambique.

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:19.790
Every minute we're
just uploading

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.110
diagnostic information.

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:25.250
Everything from user
interfaces to here

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:27.860
we've got the latitude
and longitude of the GPS,

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.490
and importantly, the cell phone
diagnostic information-- signal

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:32.880
strength.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:34.640
And then we do a
survey of the seven

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:36.680
surrounding base
stations, looking

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:39.470
at what the signal strength
is of the surrounding network

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:40.260
as well.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:41.810
FELIPE HOFFA: All of these
devices that you have deployed

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:43.394
are reporting to you
what's happening.

00:10:43.394 --> 00:10:44.434
DALE HUMBY: That's right.

00:10:44.434 --> 00:10:45.420
Right where they are.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:48.590
So if a client phones up and
says my phone isn't connecting

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:52.380
or my device isn't connecting,
I can't use your system,

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.420
then we can do an
investigation and understand,

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:58.012
is it a signal problem or
is there some other problem?

00:10:58.012 --> 00:10:59.970
FELIPE HOFFA: And you
are getting this data now

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:02.780
into Kubernetes,
or how are you--

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:04.610
DALE HUMBY: Yeah,
so all the data

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:07.370
is uploaded from the
devices to our service.

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:11.180
Some of it is in App Engine and
some of it is in Kubernetes.

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:15.850
And from there, it's
streamed live into BigQuery.

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:18.720
That's what we use for
some of our analyses.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:22.250
So if I look at selecting
the data from one

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:24.350
of our clients for
today, we've got

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.950
a ton of data that's
been uploaded already.

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:29.120
And I can go and
look specifically

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:32.360
for something like
this GSM environment.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:35.402
So I go [INAUDIBLE]--

00:11:35.402 --> 00:11:37.110
FELIPE HOFFA: That's
the GSM environment.

00:11:37.110 --> 00:11:39.190
DALE HUMBY: GSM
environment for one

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:40.610
of our clients in Mozambique.

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:43.830
Now I can have a look
at all of today's data.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:45.800
And what we've got is
this raw diagnostics

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.130
that's been uploaded.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:49.790
But unless you're a
mobile network engineer,

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:52.140
this doesn't mean a
lot to you, right?

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:52.640
Yeah.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:53.960
This is in BigQuery.

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:58.420
And then I can use
BiqQuery's just as in JSON.

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:01.280
I can use JSON methods
within BigQuery.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:03.200
I can split on commas.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:06.290
I can pull out very specific
pieces of this information,

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:09.140
like this is the country
code and the network code

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:12.170
and the base station IDs, as
well as all the signal strength

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:13.580
for all these base stations.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:15.150
FELIPE HOFFA: So
what I see, you have

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:18.359
like a big JSON blob of whatever
the devices are sending?

00:12:18.359 --> 00:12:19.400
DALE HUMBY: That's right.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:20.191
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:12:20.191 --> 00:12:21.687
That's good modeling?

00:12:21.687 --> 00:12:23.270
DALE HUMBY: Yeah,
so a lot of our data

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:25.850
is JSON, especially for
this particular table

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:27.350
which is just our event log.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:28.770
It just takes raw data.

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:31.010
There's no ETL or
anything on it.

00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:33.740
Just whatever the device
or the engineers who

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:35.520
build these devices
or developers that

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:37.820
make the app just want
to send up, we send up,

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:39.140
and we put it into BigQuery.

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:41.450
And then later on, once
we've got all the data,

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:45.380
we can decide what sort of
questions we want answered.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:48.920
And because it's in JSON and
BigQuery can operate on JSON so

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:52.310
nicely, we can extract just
the pieces of information

00:12:52.310 --> 00:12:54.815
that we want and build
new data tables over that.

00:12:54.815 --> 00:12:55.606
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:12:55.606 --> 00:12:57.650
So you can parse all
of that in real time

00:12:57.650 --> 00:12:59.210
with whatever you want to focus.

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:00.360
DALE HUMBY: That's right.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:05.760
Then from there, what I've
done is I've pulled out--

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:10.437
each row here has got seven
diagnostic readings in it.

00:13:10.437 --> 00:13:12.770
FELIPE HOFFA: So it's whatever
the modem is telling you.

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:13.170
DALE HUMBY: Yes.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:15.050
It's telling me all these
signal strengths and the base

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:15.550
stations.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:17.780
So what I did is I
ran one of the queries

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:21.800
to extract from JSON and got
the time, the device that

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.820
uploaded it, the area--

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:28.680
so that's like the suburb, I
guess-- the suburb ID, and then

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:31.100
the cell tower that it's
connected to, as well

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:32.420
as the signal strength.

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:36.020
The closer it is to zero is the
better the signal strength is.

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.402
If it's at minus
100, it's pretty bad.

00:13:38.402 --> 00:13:40.193
You're not going to be
able to do anything.

00:13:40.193 --> 00:13:42.100
FELIPE HOFFA: So the
device knows to what cell

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:42.560
tower it's connected--

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:43.560
DALE HUMBY: That's exactly it.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:45.143
FELIPE HOFFA: But
it has no idea where

00:13:45.143 --> 00:13:46.380
the cell tower is located.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:47.450
DALE HUMBY: That's right.

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:49.860
So if a client phones
us up and says,

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.190
hey, I don't have good signal.

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:53.980
Can you see what the problem is?

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:56.870
It allows us to have
a look at the device

00:13:56.870 --> 00:13:59.750
and have a look at the signal
strength that it's receiving,

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:05.920
as well as, then, join that
to the OpenCellID tower.

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:09.710
So given this, I'm
doing a join on the cell

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:13.640
ID between the towers databases
and our devices database,

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:14.750
and being able to join.

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:17.036
And what we get
is, for each cell,

00:14:17.036 --> 00:14:18.410
we get the latitude
and longitude

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:21.560
of where that base station
is as well as the maximum,

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:23.900
the minimum, and the
average signal strength

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:27.060
that our clients are
experiencing on that cell

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:28.520
tower.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:31.370
And from that, we can
put it into a dashboard.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:34.550
And this is all the
cell towers that we've

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:36.890
connected to in Maputo.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.330
That's the capital
of Mozambique.

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:40.755
FELIPE HOFFA: Is this Redash?

00:14:40.755 --> 00:14:42.005
DALE HUMBY: Yes, it is Redash.

00:14:42.005 --> 00:14:42.310
FELIPE HOFFA: Oh, good.

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:43.420
I love Redash.

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:47.770
DALE HUMBY: It's super for doing
this sort of geo-visualization.

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:51.376
So you see largely this
is green, which is good.

00:14:51.376 --> 00:14:53.875
It means that the cell towers
have got good signal strength.

00:14:53.875 --> 00:14:54.340
FELIPE HOFFA: In the
capital of Mozambique.

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:56.690
DALE HUMBY: In the capital
of Mozambique, yeah.

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:59.080
But this one device that
I showed you earlier--

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:00.200
FELIPE HOFFA: And
these are your devices.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:01.910
DALE HUMBY: These are
our devices, yeah.

00:15:01.910 --> 00:15:04.280
If we look at this guy--

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:08.480
he's in Maputo, but he
is sort of a little bit

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.140
to the north of the city.

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:12.680
So we have a look in
Redash here, and the north

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:14.965
of the city where he is--

00:15:14.965 --> 00:15:16.730
it's a bit of yellow and red.

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:19.530
So we can explore down
and see that there

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:23.870
is a cell phone tower that
has not got a great signal

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:24.740
strength.

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:28.220
And because we've
got the cell tower,

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:30.410
we can then phone up
the mobile network

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:32.760
and say that, in
this particular area,

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:35.000
this base station has
got very poor signal

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:36.470
strength for our users.

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:37.220
Could they fix it?

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:37.970
FELIPE HOFFA: Wow.

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.259
So you can use your own
data to tell the networks

00:15:40.259 --> 00:15:41.300
where they have problems?

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:44.850
DALE HUMBY: Yes, because they're
not going to report it to you.

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:49.430
But if we can give
them data, then they

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:50.485
have fixed stuff before.

00:15:50.485 --> 00:15:53.110
FELIPE HOFFA: Well, I would love
to learn more about how you're

00:15:53.110 --> 00:15:55.850
managing micropayments
all over Africa,

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:58.762
but let's save that
for another episode.

00:15:58.762 --> 00:15:59.720
DALE HUMBY: Absolutely.

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:01.130
FELIPE HOFFA: Excellent.

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:02.964
Thank you, everyone,
for joining us.

00:16:02.964 --> 00:16:03.630
Thank you, Dale.

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:05.100
DALE HUMBY: Thank
you for having me.

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:06.808
FELIPE HOFFA: And
everyone, stay curious.

00:16:06.808 --> 00:16:07.980
I'll see you soon.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:11.630
